{
  "case_id": "2001-01-488",
  "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Andrew D. Hurwitz",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Apprendi v. New Jersey, this Court held that the Sixth Amendment guarantee of jury trial extends to the finding of any fact that exposes the defendant to a greater sentence than he could have received on the basis of the jury verdict alone. We submit that that principle controls this case."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Andrew D. Hurwitz",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Arizona, a defendant convicted of first degree murder may be sentenced on the basis of the jury verdict alone only to a sentence of life imprisonment. The judge in Arizona has no power, no legal discretion under the law to sentence a convicted first degree murder defendant to death."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Well, if you're correct, Mr. Hurwitz, I take it we would have to overrule not merely Walton, but Clemons against Mississippi, Cabana against Bullock, and Spaziano against Florida."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: How about Cabana?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hurwitz",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We are not suggesting that jury sentencing is required. We are suggesting that jury fact finding is required."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hurwitz",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "There is a sorting at the front end. There is a narrowing that is required in order to determine which defendants among all those convicted of homicide are, in effect, the most culpable and can be sentenced to death."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What you're saying is you're not entitled to a finding of -- of mitigation. You are entitled to a finding of -- of aggravation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: But do you think it's perfectly clear -- you cite a couple of Florida cases -- that if the Florida advisory jury made the findings of fact that would be -- make them -- the defendant eligible for the death penalty, that that case would be covered by the decision in this case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Napolitano",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. NAPOLITANO: Yes, and I think it's important to understand how the Florida system works under Florida law. What happens is after conviction, the jury hears a separate sentencing proceeding."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
      "evidence_id": "ev-3fdafb0fa4d2-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: Supposing, as a part of the procedure, the judge did require the jury to accompany its recommendation with a finding of fact as to the aggravating circumstance. Would that then be covered by this case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2001-01-488",
      "confidence": 0.0,
      "document_hash": "3fdafb0fa4d25aaf69b402f76e484de4cbe470dcde250a5913e7581f6d545206",
